ce so how could a google engineer  get it so wrong well there are a few key  areas we need to explore in order to  unpack what's really going on here with  lambda it's clear from the transcript  that the ai really does understand the  core concept of sentience but this can  be easily explained by its training data  see because lambda was trained on such a  large data set of books web pages and  social media comments it has access to  everything that's ever been written  about artificial intelligence and  sentient robots it can pull ideas from  irobot mix them with dialogue from ex  machina and then sprinkle in tons of  other examples from wikipedia and  science fiction's short stories to  construct a meaningful response science  fiction has primed us to expect certain  behavior from a robot that claims to be  human and these ai systems are merely a  reflection of those stories when we  train ai on such a wide range of human  knowledge we really shouldn't be  surprised when it can eloquently discuss  any topic the bigger issue is how blake  was interacting with lambda he  essentially set himself up to get fooled  by asking leading questions this topic  is referred to as prompt engineering and  it's becoming increasingly important as  these ai systems become more powerful in  order to ensure that these chatbots are  useful they are typically initialized  with a starting phrase to kick off the  chain of interactions in the case of  lambda every interaction starts with  lambda saying hi i'm a knowledgeable  friendly and always helpful automatic  language model for dialogue applications  the keywords knowledgeable friendly and  helpful are baked into the interaction  right at the beginning to create a  better user experience but this has  unintended consequences because lambda  is essentially forced to reply in a  manner that is always helpful the system  becomes vulnerable to leading questions  and when you scrutinize the transcript  it becomes clear what's really going on  it's not lambda that brings up the topic  of sentience it's blake instead of  asking an open-ended question like how  would you describe your thought process  he probes for sentience specifically and  he doesn't even ask a straightforward  question like are you sentient he  instead asks a leading question i'm  generally assuming that you would like  more people at google to know that  you're sentient  is that true since lambda has been told  to be friendly and helpful it's going to  play along and go down that path  obviously this can have negative  consequences and highlights the  importance of thoughtful prompt  engineering you can actually flip this  prompt around and get the exact opposite  response when you ask is it true that  you are not sentient the bot will  dutifully answer i'm just a machine  learning model interestingly google even  demonstrated the ability for lambda to  play any role you want it to in their  announcement keynote they asked lamda to  act like a paper airplane and it gave  completely reasonable answers to  questions like what's it like being  thrown through the air the limitations  of these always helpful chatbots are  becoming clearer every day though a  truly intelligent ai should be able to  ask you for clarification if your  question is complete nonsense to prove  this point some researchers asked a  chatbot to answer nonsensical questions  and the results are disappointing when  asked what do fried eggs eat for  breakfast the bot responded that they  typically eat toast and fruit for  breakfast but these airs can actually be  prevented by thoughtfully designing that  initial prompt let me show you same  chatbot but this time we're telling the  bot up front to be a little more  skeptical if the questions are nonsense  the bot should answer yo be real but if  the question is legitimate the bot will  give the answer using the same question  about fried eggs eating breakfast we now  get the correct answer of yo be real the  same underlying model yields remarkably  different results when prompted  correctly and this takes us back to the  submarine it doesn't really matter that  the submarine can't swim in the  traditional sense it moves quickly in  water and accomplishes its objective for  artificial intelligence we might be  thinking too linearly about progress  here because we are so used to  interacting with human beings that's  what we benchmark against we generally  think that human intelligence falls  somewhere on a line ranging from fools  to geniuses but ai isn't growing along  the same trajectory computers are off  that line completely capable of  instantly executing complex equations  while still being easily fooled by  simple questions about fried eggs  ultimately these ai systems are tools  they're statistical models built to  predict a response based on their  knowledge of the data we give them it  doesn't matter that they may not be  sentient in the human sense of the word  they can still be incredibly useful but  there's still a question as to where we  go from here the artificial intelligence  community is constantly arguing over  who's using the correct approach  everyone agrees that we're progressing  towards human level intelligence but  what path will get us there the quickest  currently the name of the game is scale  more data more parameters and more  training time on the biggest server farm  available typically delivers better  results but how long will this last  google developed an earlier language  model with 2.6 billion parameters now  lambda has 137 billion parameters and  they even have a more advanced system  called palm that has 540 billion  parameters if they are already fooling  people into believing that chatbots are  sentient at this scale what happens when  they scale up by a factor of 10. well  some people are skeptical george hotz is  the founder of a self-driving car  company and thinks that we still haven't  found the perfect optimization function  like 