g by the time gpt3 launched  the architecture was obsolete the  training method was simplistic and the  data set wasn't even particularly large  nevertheless by scaling the number of  parameters to 175 billion gpt3 was now  capable of much more than simple  language generation the model could  solve math problems do translation and  unscramble anagrams even though it had  no specialized training for years ai  researchers had argued that deep  learning was all about scale and gpt 3  was proving that previously intractable  problems could be solved through scale  alone this is called the scaling  hypothesis and the idea is basically  that we've solved the architectural  problem of building human level ai we  have the algorithms we just need to feed  in more computing power and data the  pace of play here really can't be  understated when we analyze  technological progress moore's law is  typically a useful benchmark the idea is  that the power of computers generally  doubles every two years that was the  case with neural networks for decades  from the 1960s all the way to 2010 the  size of these ai systems would generally  double every two years but then  something changed we're now in the  modern era of deep learning and models  are doubling in size roughly every three  months if the scaling hypothesis is  proven correct these models could be  extremely reliable in just a few years a  war is unfolding between every tech  company to train the biggest model and  get the best results only time will tell  if they hit a wall and need to explore a  new strategy this area is still hotly  debated some researchers think all we  need is a small shift in the way we  build these systems while others think  we're going down a dead-end path in  order to predict how this will play out  in the future we have to look back at  the history of artificial intelligence  so if you want to know how all of this  got started just go watch this video  next thanks a lot

